TP.register({id:"lp9-g4",lp:9,grade:"4",mode:"baseline",nextGrade:"5th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const FLU_TITLE="The Boathouse Count";const FLU=["The boathouse at Pine Lake camp is a long shed with one wide door. Its dirt floor is worn smooth by forty summers of bare feet.","Idris had the evening count. At six o'clock he walked the racks with a clipboard. He marked off every canoe, every paddle, and every life jacket that had come back in. Anything missing got a note and a time beside it.","Most evenings the count took four minutes. The racks were full and the paddles stood upright in their barrels. The jackets hung by size along a row of wooden pegs.","The list itself was two columns in a spiral notebook, one for what went out and one for what came back. The fiddliest part of the job was writing small enough to fit a whole week onto a single page.","Twice all summer the number came up short. Both times the answer turned out to be the same. Somebody had carried a paddle up to the dining hall and leaned it against the wall by the door. Idris learned to look there first. After that the count took three minutes."].join("\n\n");const STORY_TITLE="Second Paddle";const STORY=["Kwame had been the stern paddler in the second canoe for two summers, and he had never once sat in front.","The stern is the back seat. From there you steer, and you can see everything: the bow paddler's shoulders, the whole line of the shore, the place where the wind starts to wrinkle the water a hundred feet ahead. Kwame liked knowing before anybody else did.","On the last morning of camp the counselor moved him to the bow. “Marguerite steers today,” she said. “You pull.”","In the bow you see nothing but lake. Kwame could not tell when the canoe was about to turn, and twice his paddle came down in the middle of a stroke that was already finished. His shoulders burned.","Halfway to the point he stopped trying to guess and started listening instead. Marguerite's paddle made a small sound at the end of every stroke, a soft knock against the gunwale. He matched it. After that the canoe moved like one long animal, and the point came up sooner than he expected.","At the dock Marguerite said, “You are a good bow.” Kwame turned around to coil the rope so nobody would see him grinning, which did not work at all."].join("\n\n");const T1={title:"The Clear Water Rule",src:"from <i>Lakes and the Land Around Them</i>, a book by Hollis Nakamura",body:"A lake is a bowl at the bottom of its watershed. Almost everything that lands on the hills around it arrives sooner or later. Rain carries soil off a bare slope. It carries soap off a dish pan and plant food off a lawn.\n\nOne of the things rain carries is a mineral called phosphorus. The lake holds tiny floating plants called algae. They grow faster when there is more phosphorus in the water. A little algae is food for the whole lake. A lot of algae turns the water a cloudy green. That blocks the sunlight the plants on the lake bottom need.\n\nHow much phosphorus reaches the water is mostly decided on land. That is why lake rules so often turn out to be rules about soap, soil, and lawns."};const T2={title:"Reading a Secchi Disk",src:"“Reading a Secchi Disk,” a magazine article by Yusuf Adeyemi",body:"Clarity is how far down into the water you can see. It is measured with a plain white disk about eight inches across. The disk hangs from a rope marked in tenths of a meter. A reader lowers it over the shady side of a boat until it disappears, then raises it until it comes back into view. The reader writes down the depth halfway between.\n\nThat number is the clarity reading. A reading of 4.5 meters means the disk vanished about four and a half meters down.\n\nReaders take the measurement on the same days each month all summer. One reading says very little on its own. A season of readings shows whether the water is getting clearer or cloudier. A row of summers shows whether anything the town is doing on land is working."};const T3={title:"Ten Summers of Readings",src:"“Ten Summers of Readings,” a science website page by Beatrix Sandoval",body:"Pine Lake has been read with a disk on the same two days each month for ten years. The numbers wander from month to month, but the average for each summer has moved in one direction: 4.6 meters in the first summer, 3.1 meters in the tenth.\n\nOver those same ten years the town paved two gravel roads in the watershed, and a farm at the north end stopped leaving a planted strip along its creek. Readings on their own cannot say which change mattered. They can say when the water began to cloud, and that is where anyone looking for a cause starts."};const SHORE_READ=["How Camp Fixed Its Shoreline.","For years the bank in front of the dining hall was bare dirt. Every heavy rain carried a little more of it into the lake, and after a storm the water near the dock stayed brown for two days.","Last summer the camp took that bank apart and rebuilt it. The crew hauled flat stone into a set of low steps, so that feet had one good place to go, and then planted the strip on either side with sedges and shrubs whose roots hold soil.","The work took seventy-eight hours, spread over six weeks, and most of those hours went to planting and hauling rather than to building the steps.","The bank looks rougher than it did before. It also stays where it is. After the first big rain this June, the water by the dock was clear again the next morning."].join("\n\n");const WORDS=["unbuckled","disappearance","reflection","measurement","overloaded","unreachable","shoreline","transplanted","incomplete","direction","splandorous","mistrailable"];const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper. When you finish, hold it up or read it to your teacher.";const bullets=(...x)=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${x.map(s=>`<li>${s}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const pills=(...w)=>`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:18px;gap:18px;flex-wrap:wrap">${w.map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.4rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;const group=(inner,label)=>`<div style="text-align:center">${inner}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:4px 0 0">${label}</p></div>`;const lookBack=(label,inner)=>`<details class="look-back" open><summary>Look back at ${label}</summary>${inner}</details>`;const twoTexts=()=>`<div class="texts">${[T1,T2].map((t,i)=>`<article><h3>Text ${i+1}: ${t.title}</h3><p class="small" style="margin:0 0 6px">${t.src}</p>${t.body.split(/\n\s*\n/).map(p=>`<p>${p}</p>`).join("")}</article>`).join("")}</div>`;const threeTexts=()=>`<div class="texts">${[T1,T2,T3].map((t,i)=>`<article><h3>Text ${i+1}: ${t.title}</h3>${t.body.split(/\n\s*\n/).map(p=>`<p>${p}</p>`).join("")}</article>`).join("")}</div>`;const ROPE_TABLE=()=>F.table(["Meters of rope","Centimeters of rope"],[["1","100"],["3","?"],["4","?"],["6","?"]],{caption:"Rope conversion table"});const SHORE_TABLE=()=>F.table(["What the shoreline crew did","Why it was done that way"],[["Took the bare bank apart and rebuilt it","Every heavy rain was carrying the dirt into the lake"],["Hauled flat stone into a set of low steps","Feet need one good place to go, instead of wearing a new path"],["Planted sedges and shrubs on both sides of the steps","Their roots hold the soil when it rains"],["Spent most of the seventy-eight hours planting and hauling","The planting is the part that keeps working after the crew leaves"],["Left the bank looking rougher than before","A rough bank with roots in it stays where it is"]],{caption:"The shoreline project, hour by hour"});const RAIN_TABLE=()=>F.table(["Day","Rain gauge in inches"],[["Monday","1.9"],["Tuesday","1.09"],["Wednesday","1.45"],["Thursday","1.5"]],{caption:"Rain gauge readings"});const SHAPES=[["A",[[8,25],[92,25],[92,75],[8,75]]],["B",[[12,20],[62,20],[95,88],[12,88]]],["C",[[8,85],[35,18],[92,18],[65,85]]],["D",[[10,88],[78,88],[95,40]]],["E",[[14,86],[14,24],[86,86]]]];const shapeRow=()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:18px;align-items:flex-end">${SHAPES.map(s=>group(F.poly(s[1],{size:168,alt:"Flat trail sign "+s[0]}),"Sign "+s[0])).join("")}</div>`;const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Crate Label Words",short:"Label Words",parts:[{std:["RF.4.3"],kind:"words",words:WORDS,n:12,dur:85,dir:"Read each word out loud. The last two are made-up words. Just sound them out.",desc:"reading longer words using prefixes, suffixes, roots, and syllable chunks",key:["Read each word in order. splandorous and mistrailable are made-up words, so the student has to use chunks and word parts instead of memory: splan-dor-ous, mis-trail-a-ble.","Watch for the prefixes un-, dis-, over-, trans-, in-, and mis-, the suffixes -ed, -ance, -tion, -ment, -able, and -ous, and the compound word shoreline, which breaks into two words the student already knows.","Watch whether the student breaks a long word into syllable chunks (dis-ap-pear-ance, meas-ure-ment, un-reach-a-ble, trans-plant-ed) or guesses from the first few letters."]}]},{id:2,title:"Read the Boathouse Story",short:"Read Aloud",parts:[{std:["RF.4.4"],kind:"read",n:0,label:"words read correctly",dur:110,dir:"Read the story out loud. Use your best reading voice.",desc:"a grade-level story read aloud",html:()=>F.passage(FLU,FLU_TITLE),key:["__WC__ words, not counting the title. Time 1 minute and mark errors and self-corrections on your own copy. Let the student finish the passage.","Spring grade 4 benchmark: about 133 words correct per minute, from the Hasbrouck and Tindal 2017 spring 50th percentile. For the 25th and 10th percentile rows, read them off your own copy of that table rather than from here.","Note pace, phrasing, and expression. Listen for the pauses at the commas in the long sentence about what he marked off, and for the drop in pace at the last two short sentences."]}]},{id:3,title:"Story Questions",short:"Story Qs",parts:[{title:"Prove It",std:["RL.4.1"],dur:110,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud and point to the details that prove your answer.",desc:"story questions answered with details and examples from the text",html:()=>F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true),qs:["How long had Kwame been the stern paddler, and how often had he sat in front? Use a detail from the story.","Why did Kwame like the stern? Give two details from the story.","What went wrong for Kwame in the bow at first? Use details from the story.","What did he do to fix it? Use details from the story.","How do you know Kwame was pleased at the dock? Use the story to explain."],key:["Two summers, and he had never once sat in front. The story says both in the first sentence. (RL.4.1, explicit)","Any two: you steer from the stern; you can see the bow paddler's shoulders, the whole line of the shore, and the place where the wind starts to wrinkle the water a hundred feet ahead; and he liked knowing before anybody else did. (RL.4.1, details)","He could not tell when the canoe was about to turn, and twice his paddle came down in the middle of a stroke that was already finished. (RL.4.1, explicit)","He stopped trying to guess and listened instead, then matched the soft knock Marguerite's paddle made against the gunwale at the end of every stroke. (RL.4.1, explicit)","He turned around to coil the rope so nobody would see him grinning, and the story adds that it did not work at all, so he was grinning. Marguerite had just told him he was a good bow. (RL.4.1, inference backed by details)"]},{title:"Word Work",std:["RL.4.4"],dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud. Tell which words in the story helped you.",desc:"the meaning of words and phrases as they are used in a story",html:()=>lookBack("the story",F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true)),qs:["What does stern mean in this story? What in the story tells you?","What does bow mean? Use the story.","What does gunwale mean? Use the story.","The story says the wind starts to wrinkle the water. Is the water really wrinkled? What does that mean?","“His shoulders burned.” Were they on fire? What does this really tell you?","“The canoe moved like one long animal.” What does that tell you about how the canoe was moving?"],key:["The back seat of the canoe, where the steering is done. The story says so in the second paragraph and adds that from there you can see the bow paddler ahead of you. (RL.4.4)","The front seat. In the bow you see nothing but lake, and from the stern you look at the bow paddler's shoulders, so the bow has to be in front. (RL.4.4)","The top edge of the canoe's side. The paddle knocks softly against it at the end of each stroke, so it has to be the edge the paddle brushes on the way out of the water. (RL.4.4)","No. It means small ripples start crossing water that was smooth, the way creases cross a flat cloth. The wind is what starts them. (RL.4.4, nonliteral phrase)","No. His shoulders ached and felt hot from pulling hard, over and over, without a rest. (RL.4.4, nonliteral phrase)","It is a comparison using the word like. Both paddlers were finally pulling together, so the canoe moved in one long smooth motion instead of in jerks. (RL.4.4, comparison in a text)"]}]},{id:4,title:"Two Lake Texts",short:"Two Texts",parts:[{title:"Use the Text",std:["RI.4.1"],dur:105,dir:"Read both texts to yourself. Then answer out loud using details from the texts.",desc:"informational text questions answered with details and examples from the text",html:()=>twoTexts(),qs:["What is a clarity reading? Use details from Text 2.","How is the rope on the disk marked? Use the text.","What does a lot of algae do to a lake? Use details from Text 1.","Why do readers measure on the same days every month all summer? Use the text.","Text 1 says lake rules often turn out to be rules about soap, soil, and lawns. Why would that be? Use details from the text."],key:["How far down into the water you can see. The reader lowers the white disk until it disappears, raises it until it comes back into view, and writes down the depth halfway between those two points. (RI.4.1, explicit)","In tenths of a meter. (RI.4.1, explicit)","It turns the water a cloudy green and blocks the sunlight that the plants on the lake bottom need. (RI.4.1, explicit)","Because one reading says very little on its own. A season of readings shows whether the water is getting clearer or cloudier, and a row of summers shows whether what the town does on land is working. (RI.4.1, explicit)","Because the phosphorus that feeds the algae arrives from the land. Rain carries soil off a bare slope, soap off a dish pan, and plant food off a lawn, and the text says how much reaches the water is mostly decided on land. (RI.4.1, inference backed by details)"]},{title:"Put It Together",std:["RI.4.9"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Answer out loud. Use a fact from each text in every answer.",desc:"putting together information from two texts on the same topic",html:()=>lookBack("the two texts",twoTexts()),qs:["A lake's clarity readings get smaller summer after summer. Use both texts to explain what is probably happening in the water and where to look for the cause.","A reader takes one reading in August and says the lake is in trouble. Use Text 2 to explain what is wrong with saying that.","Which text would you use to explain how the number is measured. Which to explain why the number changes? Tell why."],key:["From Text 1: more phosphorus means more algae, and a lot of algae turns the water a cloudy green, so smaller readings point to more algae in the water. From Text 2: clarity is how far down you can see, and a row of summers is what shows a real change. The cause is on land, where the phosphorus comes from. Credit an answer that uses at least one fact from each text. (RI.4.9)","Text 2 says one reading says very little on its own, because the numbers move around. It takes a season of readings to show a direction and a row of summers to show whether anything has really changed. (RI.4.9)","Text 2 for how it is measured, because it is the only one that describes the disk, the rope marked in tenths of a meter, and the halfway depth. Text 1 for why the number changes, because it explains phosphorus, algae, and cloudy green water. (RI.4.9)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Clear Water Notes",short:"Notes",parts:[{std:["W.4.8"],n:3,label:"checks met",dur:95,dir:"Read the two sources. Write your notes on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"taking notes in their own words, sorting the notes, and listing the sources",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">You are gathering facts for a report on why a lake's water goes cloudy.</p>${twoTexts()}<ol class="qlist"><li>Write three notes. Use both sources. Keep them short and use your own words, not the sentences from the page.</li><li>Sort your notes. Write a heading above each one: How Clarity Is Measured, or What Clouds the Water.</li><li>List your sources. Next to each note, write the title of the source it came from.</li></ol>`,key:["Three short notes drawn from both sources, in the student's own words and not copied sentences. Examples: rain carries soil, soap, and plant food off the land; phosphorus makes algae grow faster; too much algae turns the water green and blocks the light; clarity is how far down you can see; the rope is marked in tenths of a meter; one reading on its own means little. (W.4.8, gathers information, takes notes, paraphrases)","Each note sits under the heading that fits. The disk, the marked rope, the halfway depth, and the monthly schedule go under How Clarity Is Measured. Rain off the land, phosphorus, algae, and green water go under What Clouds the Water. (W.4.8, categorizes)","Writes both titles next to the right notes: The Clear Water Rule, and “Reading a Secchi Disk.” Accept the authors as well. (W.4.8, provides a list of sources)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Listen to the Shoreline Report",short:"Listen",parts:[{std:["SL.4.2"],n:5,dur:100,dir:"Listen while your teacher reads. Then put the answers in your own words. For the last two, use the graph.",desc:"paraphrasing a text read aloud and information presented quantitatively",read:SHORE_READ,html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Your teacher will read you a short report about one summer's work at camp. Listen, then say it back in your own words. After that, look at the graph.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.barGraph({title:"Hours on the shoreline project",cats:["Planting","Hauling","Steps"],vals:[36,24,18],max:40,step:5}),"Hours spent planting, hauling stone, and building the steps"]),qs:["Put the report in your own words. What was the problem, and what did camp do about it?","Paraphrase the part about the plants. Why do the roots matter?","How long did the work take, and how was it spread out?","Look at the graph. Say in your own words what it shows.","Which job took the most hours and which took the fewest? Give the numbers."],key:["Paraphrases rather than repeats: the bank by the dining hall was bare dirt, every heavy rain washed some of it into the lake and clouded the water by the dock, so last summer a crew rebuilt the bank with stone steps and planting. (SL.4.2, paraphrases a text read aloud)","Paraphrases: the crew planted sedges and shrubs beside the steps, and their roots hold the soil in place so the rain cannot carry it into the lake. (SL.4.2, paraphrases a portion of the text)","Seventy-eight hours in all, spread over six weeks, with most of the hours going to planting and hauling rather than to building the steps. (SL.4.2, supporting detail)","Paraphrases the graph: the crew's hours were split three ways, and planting took far more of them than either of the other two jobs. (SL.4.2, information presented quantitatively)","Planting took the most at 36 hours. The steps took the fewest at 18. Hauling stone was 24. The three bars add to 78, the same total as the report gave, and planting took exactly twice as many hours as the steps. (SL.4.2, detail presented quantitatively)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Lookout Report",short:"Report",parts:[{std:["SL.4.4"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:85,dir:"Take one minute to plan. Then talk for about a minute. Speak clearly.",desc:"reporting on a topic in an organized way, with facts and descriptive details",html:()=>`<p class="huge" aria-hidden="true">${F.icon("mountain",96)}</p><p class="big">Pick one and report on it. Tell me enough that I could tell somebody else about it.</p>${bullets("A place outdoors you know well","Something you know how to do outdoors")}${pills("Main idea","Facts","Details","In order")}`,key:["Reports in an organized order instead of jumping around (SL.4.4)","Gives facts that fit the topic and support one main idea (SL.4.4)","Adds relevant, descriptive details that help the listener picture it (SL.4.4)","Speaks clearly at a pace you can follow (SL.4.4)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Fix the Camp Log",short:"Grammar",parts:[{std:["L.4.1"],n:6,dur:90,dir:"Say each line out loud the correct way.",desc:"spoken grammar items (relative adverb, progressive tense, modal words, order of adjectives, fragments, confused words)",qs:["The cove ____ we launch the canoes is out of the wind. (where or which)","Right now the loons ____ out past the reeds. (call) Start with are.","Pick the better word: The bag is not closed yet, so we ____ turn it over. (must not or might not)","Fix the order of these words: wooden / four / long / paddles","Fix this fragment so it is a whole sentence: Because the lake was flat.","Finish each blank with to, too, or two. The ____ of us carried the canoe ____ the dock. It was ____ heavy to lift alone."],key:["where. It points back to a place, so it takes a relative adverb, not which. (L.4.1, relative adverb)","are calling. (L.4.1, progressive verb tense)","must not. The bag is open, so turning it over is not allowed, not just unlikely. (L.4.1, modal auxiliary)","four long wooden paddles. Number, then size, then what it is made of, then the noun. (L.4.1, ordering adjectives)","Any correct fix that adds a main clause, such as: We paddled to the point because the lake was flat. Or: Because the lake was flat, we paddled to the point. (L.4.1, correcting a fragment)","two, to, too. (L.4.1, frequently confused words)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Place Value on the Tally",short:"Place Value",parts:[{std:["4.NBT.A.1"],n:5,dur:70,dir:"Look at the number on the boathouse board. Answer out loud and explain how you know.",desc:"a digit worth 10 times the same digit one place to its right",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Camp keeps a running tally of paddle strokes on the long crossing. Here is the number on the board.</p>`,fig:()=>`<p class="huge" style="margin:0">3,300</p><p class="small" style="text-align:center;margin-top:0">paddle strokes counted across the lake</p>`,qs:["In 3,300, how does the 3 in the thousands place compare to the 3 in the hundreds place?","A 7 in the tens place is worth 70. In which place would a 7 be worth 10 times as much. What would it be worth there?","True or silly: in 4,400 the 4 in the hundreds place is worth 10 times as much as the 4 in the thousands place. If it is silly, fix it.","Finish this sentence: 8,000 is ____ times as much as 800.","In 55,500, how does the 5 in the ten thousands place compare to the 5 in the thousands place?"],key:["It is worth 10 times as much. The 3 in the thousands place is 3,000 and the 3 in the hundreds place is 300, and 3,000 is 10 times 300. Partial: naming the two values with no comparison is approaching. (4.NBT.A.1)","The hundreds place, where a 7 is worth 700. 700 is 10 times 70. Partial: naming the place with no value given is approaching. (4.NBT.A.1)","Silly. It is the other way around: the 4 in the thousands place is 4,000, the 4 in the hundreds place is 400, and 4,000 is 10 times 400. Partial: saying it is silly with no fix given is approaching. (4.NBT.A.1)","10. Partial: 100 counts two places instead of one and does not count. (4.NBT.A.1)","It is worth 10 times as much. 50,000 is 10 times 5,000. Partial: naming the two values with no comparison is approaching. (4.NBT.A.1)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Round the Lake Numbers",short:"Round It",parts:[{std:["4.NBT.A.3"],n:5,dur:65,dir:"Say each answer out loud.",desc:"rounding multi-digit whole numbers to a given place",qs:["Round 37,482 to the nearest thousand.","Round 37,482 to the nearest hundred.","Round 37,482 to the nearest ten thousand.","A trail crew counted 2,650 steps around the lake loop. Round that to the nearest hundred steps.","Round 148,392 to the nearest thousand."],key:["37,000. The hundreds digit is 4, so it rounds down. Partial: 38,000 rounds up when the hundreds digit is 4 and does not count. (4.NBT.A.3)","37,500. The tens digit is 8, so it rounds up. Partial: 37,400 rounds down when the tens digit is 8 and does not count. (4.NBT.A.3)","40,000. There are 7 thousands, so it rounds up to the next ten thousand. Partial: 30,000 rounds down when the thousands digit is 7 and does not count. (4.NBT.A.3)","2,700. The tens digit is 5, which sits exactly halfway, so it rounds up. Partial: 2,600 rounds down at the halfway mark and does not count. (4.NBT.A.3)","148,000. The hundreds digit is 3, so it rounds down. Partial: 149,000 rounds up when the hundreds digit is 3 and does not count. (4.NBT.A.3)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Times As Many Canoes",short:"Times As",parts:[{std:["4.OA.A.1"],n:5,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud. For numbers 1, 3, and 4, write the equation on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"reading a multiplication equation as a comparison and writing comparisons as equations",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Camp keeps canoes at two places: a small rack at the near dock, and the big boathouse that holds the whole fleet.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.array(1,7,"square"),"Near dock: 7 canoes"],[`<p class="huge" style="margin:0">42</p>`,"The whole fleet: 42 canoes"]),qs:["The near dock holds 7 canoes and the whole fleet is 42. The fleet is how many times as many canoes? Write the multiplication equation.","Read 54 = 9 × 6 as a comparison. Say it two different ways.","A short paddle takes 15 minutes. The long paddle takes 4 times as long. Write a multiplication equation and find the time.","One rope is 8 meters long. A second rope is 5 times as long. Write the equation and find the length.","Spot the mistake. Noor says that 32 = 8 × 4 means 32 is 8 more than 4. Fix her sentence and tell her what 8 more than 4 really is."],key:["6 times as many. 42 = 6 × 7. Partial: 35 more is a difference and not a multiplicative comparison, so it does not count. (4.OA.A.1)","54 is 9 times as many as 6, and 54 is 6 times as many as 9. Partial: one of the two ways given is approaching. (4.OA.A.1)","4 × 15 = 60 minutes. Accept t = 4 × 15. Partial: the equation right with no time given is approaching. (4.OA.A.1, a verbal comparison written as a multiplication equation)","5 × 8 = 40 meters. Partial: 40 with no unit named is approaching. (4.OA.A.1)","32 is 8 times as many as 4, not 8 more than 4. 8 more than 4 is 12. Partial: fixing the sentence with no value for 8 more than 4 is approaching. (4.OA.A.1, telling a multiplicative comparison from an additive one)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Camp Measures",short:"Measures",parts:[{title:"Units",std:["4.MD.A.1"],n:6,dur:95,dir:"Say each answer out loud. For number 6, read the three missing numbers from the table.",desc:"relative sizes of measurement units and changing a larger unit into a smaller one",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Everything in the camp supply book is written in the smaller unit, so the numbers come out whole.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${ROPE_TABLE()}</div>`,qs:["A paddle is 2 meters long. How many centimeters is that?","A food crate holds 5 kilograms. How many grams is that?","The water jug holds 8 liters. How many milliliters is that?","The hike lasts 3 hours. How many minutes is that?","A dock board is 7 feet long. How many inches is that?","Finish the table. What are the three missing numbers?"],key:["200 centimeters. 1 meter is 100 centimeters, so 2 × 100 = 200. Partial: 20 uses tens instead of hundreds and does not count. (4.MD.A.1)","5,000 grams. 1 kilogram is 1,000 grams, so 5 × 1,000 = 5,000. Partial: 500 uses hundreds instead of thousands and does not count. (4.MD.A.1)","8,000 milliliters. 1 liter is 1,000 milliliters, so 8 × 1,000 = 8,000. Partial: 800 uses hundreds instead of thousands and does not count. (4.MD.A.1)","180 minutes. 1 hour is 60 minutes, so 3 × 60 = 180. Partial: 180 with no unit named is approaching. (4.MD.A.1)","84 inches. 1 foot is 12 inches, so 7 × 12 = 84. Partial: 84 with no unit named is approaching. (4.MD.A.1)","300, 400, and 600 centimeters. The pairs are 1 and 100, 3 and 300, 4 and 400, 6 and 600. Partial: two of the three missing numbers right is approaching. (4.MD.A.1, a two-column conversion table)"]},{title:"Area and Perimeter",std:["4.MD.A.3"],n:6,dur:95,dir:"Solve each one on your paper and hold it up. Say the unit with every answer.",desc:"area and perimeter formulas for rectangles in real problems",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The tent field is a rectangle. Camp measures it every spring so it knows how many tents fit and how much rope the fence takes.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.rect(18,12,"m"),"The tent field"]),qs:["The field is 18 meters long and 12 meters wide. What is its area?","What is the perimeter of the field?","A tarp has an area of 45 square feet and is 5 feet wide. How long is it?","Pad A is 8 feet by 6 feet. Pad B is 12 feet by 4 feet. Which pad has the bigger area?","Which of those two pads has the smaller perimeter, and by how much?","A rope fence runs around all four sides of the tent field. The rope comes in 5 meter pieces. How many pieces does camp need?"],key:["216 square meters. 18 × 12 = 216. Partial: 216 with no square unit named is approaching. (4.MD.A.3)","60 meters. 2 × (18 + 12) = 2 × 30 = 60. Or: 18 + 12 + 18 + 12 = 60. Partial: 30 adds only one of each side and does not count. (4.MD.A.3)","9 feet. The area formula with an unknown factor: 5 × ? = 45, so ? = 9. Partial: 9 with no unit named is approaching. (4.MD.A.3)","Neither. Both are 48 square feet, because 8 × 6 = 48 and 12 × 4 = 48. Partial: naming neither with only one area worked out is approaching. (4.MD.A.3)","Pad A, by 4 feet. Pad A is 2 × (8 + 6) = 28 feet and Pad B is 2 × (12 + 4) = 32 feet. Same area, different perimeter. Partial: naming Pad A with no difference given is approaching. (4.MD.A.3)","12 pieces. The perimeter is 60 meters and 60 ÷ 5 = 12. Partial: the perimeter right with no division done is approaching. (4.MD.A.3)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Sort the Trail Signs",short:"Signs",parts:[{std:["4.G.A.2"],n:6,dur:95,dir:"Look at the five trail signs. Answer out loud. Point to the sides or the corners you mean.",desc:"classifying two-dimensional figures by parallel sides, perpendicular sides, and angle size",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Camp cuts its trail signs in different shapes so a hiker can tell them apart from far away. Parallel sides run the same direction and never meet. Perpendicular sides meet at a square corner, which is a right angle.</p>`,fig:()=>shapeRow(),qs:["Which signs have at least one pair of parallel sides? Name them.","Which signs have perpendicular sides? Name them.","Which sign is a right triangle? How do you know?","Sign D is a triangle too. Is it a right triangle? How do you know?","Sign B has exactly one pair of parallel sides. Sign C has two pairs. What is each of those shapes called?","Name a sign that has an angle bigger than a right angle. Point to that angle."],key:["A, B, and C. Sign A is a rectangle, so both pairs of opposite sides are parallel. Sign B has one pair, the short top side and the long bottom side. Sign C is a parallelogram with two pairs. Signs D and E are triangles, and a triangle has no parallel sides. Partial: two of the three signs named is approaching. (4.G.A.2)","A, B, and E. Sign A has four square corners, so every pair of sides that meets is perpendicular. Sign B has two square corners, both on its upright left side, so that side is perpendicular to the top and to the bottom. Sign E has one square corner where the upright side meets the bottom side. Signs C and D have no square corners. Partial: two of the three signs named is approaching. (4.G.A.2)","Sign E. It is a triangle with one right angle, at the lower left corner where the upright side meets the bottom side. Partial: naming Sign E with no reason is approaching. (4.G.A.2, identifies right triangles)","No. None of Sign D's three corners is square. Its widest corner sits at the right-hand end of the flat bottom edge, where the outline turns up toward the point, and that corner is wider than a right angle. The other two corners are both narrower than a right angle. Checked with the corner of a card, not one of the three fits. Partial: saying no with no check of the corners is approaching. (4.G.A.2)","Sign B is a trapezoid and Sign C is a parallelogram. Partial: one of the two names given is approaching. (4.G.A.2)","Sign B, Sign C, or Sign D. Sign B's wider corner is the upper right one. Sign C's two wider corners are the upper left and the lower right. Sign D's one wide corner sits at the right-hand end of its flat bottom edge, where the outline turns up toward the point. Signs A and E have no angle bigger than a right angle. Partial: naming a sign with no angle pointed out is approaching. (4.G.A.2, angles of a specified size)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Lake Fractions",short:"Fractions",parts:[{title:"Equal Fractions",std:["4.NF.A.1"],n:4,dur:75,dir:"Look at the two bars. Answer out loud. Write your fractions on your paper.",desc:"explaining and making equivalent fractions",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The cooks mark how full each water jug is. Both bars below stand for the same size jug.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:26px;align-items:flex-end">${group(F.fracBar(3,2,{width:320}),"2/3")}${group(F.fracBar(9,6,{width:320}),"6/9")}</div>`,qs:["Do these two bars show the same amount? Explain using the number of parts and the size of the parts.","Write two fractions that are equal to 3/4.","Explain why 2/5 and 4/10 are the same amount.","Nadia says 3/5 = 6/15. Is she right? If not, fix it."],key:["Yes, the same amount is shaded. The second bar has 3 times as many parts and each part is 3 times smaller, so 2/3 and 6/9 cover the same amount. Partial: saying yes with nothing said about the size of the parts is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)","Any two of 6/8, 9/12, 12/16, 15/20. Partial: one equal fraction given is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)","Cutting each fifth into 2 pieces gives 10 pieces in the whole and turns the 2 shaded fifths into 4 shaded tenths. Twice as many pieces, each half the size, so the amount does not change. Partial: naming the cutting with nothing said about the pieces being smaller is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)","No. 3/5 = 6/10, not 6/15. She doubled the top but tripled the bottom, and both have to be multiplied by the same number. Partial: saying no with no correction given is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)"]},{title:"Compare Fractions",std:["4.NF.A.2"],n:5,dur:75,dir:"Show your thinking on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"comparing fractions with different numerators and denominators",qs:["Which is more of the same jug, 3/8 or 2/5? Show how you know.","Write &gt;, &lt;, or = to make it true: 5/6 ____ 7/12","Which is greater, 4/7 or 1/2? Use half as your benchmark.","Put these in order from least to greatest: 2/3, 5/8, 3/4","Two jugs are different sizes. Can you use the fractions alone to compare 1/2 of the small jug with 1/2 of the big jug? Tell why or why not."],key:["2/5. In fortieths, 3/8 is 15/40 and 2/5 is 16/40, so 2/5 is more. A drawing of two same-size bars also counts. Partial: naming 2/5 with no common denominator and no drawing is approaching. (4.NF.A.2)","5/6 &gt; 7/12. In twelfths, 5/6 is 10/12, and 10/12 is more than 7/12. Partial: the right choice with the sign the wrong way round is approaching. (4.NF.A.2)","4/7. Half of 7 is 3 1/2, so 4 sevenths is more than half. Accept 8/14 against 7/14. Partial: naming 4/7 with no benchmark work shown is approaching. (4.NF.A.2, benchmark)","5/8, 2/3, 3/4. In twenty-fourths they are 15/24, 16/24, and 18/24. Partial: two of the three in order is approaching. (4.NF.A.2)","No. Two fractions can only be compared when they refer to the same whole. The jugs are different sizes, so half of one is not the same amount as half of the other. Partial: saying no with nothing said about the same whole is approaching. (4.NF.A.2)"]}]},{id:15,title:"Rain Gauge Decimals",short:"Decimals",parts:[{title:"Fractions and Decimals",std:["4.NF.C.6"],n:6,dur:65,dir:"Write your answers on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"writing tenths and hundredths as decimals and as fractions",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The trail map is drawn on a grid of 100 equal squares. The shaded squares are the part of the camp loop that runs through the woods.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.gridRect(10,10,63,{cell:30}),"The whole trail map, 100 equal squares"]),qs:["What part of the grid is shaded? Write it as a fraction.","Write that same amount as a decimal.","Write 7/10 as a decimal.","Write 0.09 as a fraction.","The rain gauge read 4 inches and 5 tenths. Write that as a decimal, and as a whole number with a fraction.","Write 2 and 8 hundredths as a decimal."],key:["63/100. Six full rows of 10 is 60, and 3 more squares makes 63. Partial: 63 given with no fraction form is approaching. (4.NF.C.6)","0.63. Or: 63/100 is 6 tenths and 3 hundredths, which is 0.63. (4.NF.C.6)","0.7. Or: 7/10 is 70/100, which is 0.70. (4.NF.C.6)","9/100. Partial: 9/10 puts the 9 in the tenths place and does not count. (4.NF.C.6)","4.5 inches, and 4 and 5/10 inches. Accept 4 and 1/2 as well. Partial: one of the two forms given is approaching. (4.NF.C.6)","2.08. The 8 is in the hundredths place, so a zero holds the tenths place. Partial: 2.8 leaves no zero holding the tenths place and does not count. (4.NF.C.6)"]},{title:"Compare Decimals",std:["4.NF.C.7"],n:5,dur:65,dir:"Answer out loud. Tell how you know. For the last two, write your answer on your paper.",desc:"comparing decimals to hundredths",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Camp empties the rain gauge every morning and writes the reading down in inches.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${RAIN_TABLE()}</div>`,qs:["Which is more rain, 0.6 of an inch or 0.58 of an inch? How do you know?","Write &gt;, &lt;, or = to make it true: 0.40 ____ 0.4","Write &gt;, &lt;, or = to make it true: 0.07 ____ 0.7","Put the four readings in the table in order from least to greatest.","Theo says 0.12 is more than 0.9 because 12 is more than 9. What is wrong with that?"],key:["0.6 of an inch. Written to hundredths it is 0.60, which is 60 hundredths, and 0.58 is 58 hundredths. Partial: naming 0.6 with no reason from hundredths is approaching. (4.NF.C.7)","0.40 = 0.4. The extra zero in the hundredths place adds nothing. Partial: making one of them the greater counts digits instead of places and does not count. (4.NF.C.7)","0.07 &lt; 0.7. That is 7 hundredths against 70 hundredths. Partial: the right choice with the sign the wrong way round is approaching. (4.NF.C.7)","1.09, 1.45, 1.5, 1.9. Written to hundredths they are 1.09, 1.45, 1.50, and 1.90. Partial: three of the four in order is approaching. (4.NF.C.7)","The number of digits does not decide it. Compare place by place from the left: 0.12 has 1 tenth and 0.9 has 9 tenths, so 0.9 is the bigger number. In hundredths it is 12 against 90. Partial: saying the digits do not decide it with no comparison of the tenths is approaching. (4.NF.C.7)"]}]},{id:16,title:"Camp Writer",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"opinion",label:"Opinion",icon:"💬",hint:"Canoes or the shoreline path?"},std:["W.4.1","L.4.1"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:260,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an opinion writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">Camp has money for one project this summer. It can buy six more canoes, or it can rebuild the muddy path along the shore with stone steps and planting. More canoes would mean shorter waits for a turn on the lake. The new path would stop the bank washing into the water every time it rains.</p><p class="big">Which should camp pick? Write your opinion with reasons. Write 5 to 7 sentences.</p>${bullets("Introduce the topic and state your opinion clearly","Give reasons backed by facts or details","Link your opinion and reasons: because, for example, also, in order to","End with a concluding statement")}`,key:["Introduces the topic and states a clear opinion about the canoes or the path (W.4.1)","Supports the opinion with reasons, such as shorter waits for the lake, or soil staying out of the water (W.4.1)","Backs at least one reason with a fact or detail from today's texts or report (W.4.1)","Links opinion and reasons with words and phrases such as because, for example, also, in order to (W.4.1)","Ends with a concluding statement, and keeps verb tenses and sentence boundaries correct (W.4.1, L.4.1)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"facts",label:"Explain",icon:"📋",hint:"How clarity is measured"},std:["W.4.2","L.4.1"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:260,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an informative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">Write to explain how a lake's clarity is measured and why one reading is not enough to tell you anything. Use facts from the two texts and from the chart. Write 5 to 7 sentences.</p>${bullets("Introduce your topic","Give facts, details, or examples","Use the topic words correctly: clarity, reading, watershed, algae","Use linking words: for example, also, because","End with a concluding sentence")}<div class="figure">${SHORE_TABLE()}</div>${lookBack("the two texts",twoTexts())}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly (W.4.2)","Develops the topic with facts from the texts, such as the white disk about eight inches across, the rope marked in tenths of a meter, the halfway depth, readings on the same days each month, and the point that one reading says very little (W.4.2)","Uses the topic words clarity, reading, watershed, and algae correctly (W.4.2)","Links ideas with words such as for example, also, because, another (W.4.2)","Ends with a concluding statement, and keeps verb tenses and sentence boundaries correct (W.4.2, L.4.1)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"story",label:"Story",icon:"✨",hint:"First canoe out"},std:["W.4.3","L.4.1"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:260,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a narrative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">On the last morning of camp you get the front seat in the first canoe to leave the dock. The lake is flat and the mist has not lifted yet. Write the story of that paddle. It can be real or made up. Write 6 to 8 sentences.</p>${pills("Set the scene","Then","After that","By the end")}${bullets("Set the scene and tell who is there","Tell the events in a clear order, with time words","Use dialogue, or a description of what someone thinks and feels","Use words that help the reader picture the lake that morning","Give the story an ending")}`,key:["Sets up the situation and introduces the narrator or the other characters (W.4.3)","Events unfold in a clear order, helped by time words such as first, after that, by then, finally (W.4.3)","Uses dialogue or description to show what someone thinks or feels (W.4.3)","Uses concrete words and sensory detail about the lake, the mist, or the paddling (W.4.3)","Ends the story, and keeps verb tenses and sentence boundaries correct (W.4.3, L.4.1)"]}]},{id:17,title:"Otter and Dolphin",short:"Structures",parts:[{std:["4-LS1-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the two animals. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"arguing that named animal structures do a job that supports survival, growth or reproduction",html:()=>`<p class="big">A sea otter floats on its back in the kelp just off the beach. A dolphin passes further out.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("otter",104)}${F.icon("dolphin",104)}</div>
     <p class="big">The otter has the densest fur of any animal, loose skin pockets under its front legs, and flat back teeth. The dolphin has a smooth streamlined body, a blowhole on top of its head, and large lungs inside.</p>`,qs:["Name the job of the otter's dense fur and the job of its flat back teeth.","Build an argument that the dolphin's blowhole being on top of its head suits the way it lives.","The otter's loose skin pockets hold a favourite stone and food while it swims. Explain how that supports its survival."],key:["Dense fur traps a layer of air next to the skin, which holds the otter's body heat in cold water, because it has no thick blubber layer. Flat back teeth crush hard shells so it can eat clams, crabs and sea urchins. (4-LS1-1)","A dolphin breathes air but lives in water. A blowhole on top means it only has to break the surface with the top of its head to breathe, without stopping or lifting its whole face out, so it can breathe while still swimming. Look for the claim plus the reasoning. (4-LS1-1)","It can carry a stone to crack shells against and keep food with it instead of making a separate trip for each item, so it loses less energy and less food to the water or to other animals while it feeds. (4-LS1-1)"]},{std:["4-LS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read what happens, then answer. You may draw your model.",desc:"modelling the path from sense organ to brain to response in an animal",html:()=>`<p class="big">A dolphin makes a short click and listens. The sound travels out, bounces off a school of fish ahead, and comes back. The dolphin turns slightly left and speeds up.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("dolphin",104)}${F.icon("fish",104)}</div>`,qs:["Which sense took in the information, and which body part took it in?","What did the dolphin's brain work out from the returning sound?","Build a three step model of the path, from the click going out to the dolphin turning left."],key:["Hearing. The returning sound is taken in through the dolphin's head and jaw and carried to its ears. Accept hearing through the ears. (4-LS1-2)","Where the fish were and how far away: the direction the echo came from and how long it took to come back. The ears collect the sound and the brain turns it into a position. (4-LS1-2)","The click goes out and the echo comes back to the ears, the brain processes the echo and works out where the fish are and chooses a course, the body and tail carry out the turn. The brain step is what this standard is about. (4-LS1-2)"]}]},{id:18,title:"The Beach Cliff",short:"Rock Layers",parts:[{std:["4-ESS1-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the cliff behind the beach. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"reading rock layers and the fossils in them as evidence of landscape change",html:()=>`<p class="big">The cliff behind the beach shows four layers. Layers stack with the newest on top.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What the layer is</th><th>Fossils in it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>top</td><td>loose dune sand</td><td>beach grass roots</td></tr>
     <tr><td>second</td><td>sandstone with slanting layers inside it</td><td>burrows and sand dollar pieces</td></tr>
     <tr><td>third</td><td>brown mudstone</td><td>fern fronds and pine cones</td></tr>
     <tr><td>bottom</td><td>pale limestone</td><td>whole clam shells in rows</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which layer formed first, and how does the chart show it?","The third layer holds fern fronds and pine cones. What was this place when that layer formed?","Use all four layers in order to explain how this place changed over time."],key:["The pale limestone at the bottom, because layers build upward and everything above it landed later. (4-ESS1-1)","Dry land with a forest on it. Ferns and pines grow on land, so the sea was not covering this spot then. (4-ESS1-1)","A shallow sea left limestone with clams, then the sea gave way to land with a fern and pine forest in mud, then the sea returned and left sandstone with burrows and sand dollars, then dunes built up on top where beach grass grows now. The strongest answer notices the sea came and went more than once. (4-ESS1-1)"]},{std:["4-ESS2-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the measurements. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"using measurements to compare the rate of erosion under different conditions",html:()=>`<p class="big">Markers were set along the cliff top and measured each year for five years, to see how far the cliff edge moved back.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Marker</th><th>What the cliff is like there</th><th>Cliff lost in five years</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>loose sand and gravel, waves reach the base at high tide</td><td>340 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>loose sand and gravel, a wide beach keeps waves off the base</td><td>90 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>hard rock, waves reach the base at high tide</td><td>12 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>loose sand and gravel with deep rooted plants on the face</td><td>45 cm</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which pair of markers shows what the waves at the base do? Give the numbers and what they show.","Which pair shows what the kind of rock does, and what is the answer?","Work out the average loss each year at marker 1. Then say why the cliff path there needed to be moved."],key:["Markers 1 and 2, both loose sand and gravel: 340 cm where the waves reach the base and 90 cm where a wide beach keeps them off. Waves undercutting the base make the cliff retreat much faster. (4-ESS2-1)","Markers 1 and 3, both reached by waves: 340 cm in loose sand and gravel and only 12 cm in hard rock. The softer the material, the faster it erodes. (4-ESS2-1)","About 68 cm a year, which is 340 divided by 5. At that rate a path a few metres from the edge would be lost within a few years, so it had to be moved back before the ground under it went. (4-ESS2-1)"]}]},{id:19,title:"Headlands and Bays",short:"Map Patterns",parts:[{std:["4-ESS2-2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart from the coast map. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"interpreting map data to describe patterns in Earth's features",html:()=>`<p class="big">A coast map was read at six points along 30 kilometres of shoreline.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("wave",80)}${F.icon("shell",80)}${F.icon("mountain",80)}</div>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Point</th><th>Kind of rock on the map</th><th>Shape of the shoreline</th><th>Beach width</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>hard granite</td><td>sticks out into the sea</td><td>no beach</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>soft shale</td><td>curves inland</td><td>180 m of sand</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>hard granite</td><td>sticks out into the sea</td><td>narrow strip of boulders</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>soft shale</td><td>curves inland</td><td>210 m of sand</td></tr>
     <tr><td>5</td><td>hard granite</td><td>sticks out into the sea</td><td>no beach</td></tr>
     <tr><td>6</td><td>soft sandstone</td><td>curves inland</td><td>160 m of sand</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Describe the pattern linking the kind of rock to the shape of the shoreline.","Describe the pattern in the beach widths and say how it fits the first pattern.","A seventh point is found with hard granite. Predict its shoreline shape and beach width, and explain your reasoning."],key:["Hard granite always sticks out into the sea as a headland, and soft shale or sandstone always curves inland as a bay. The rock type and the shape go together at every point. (4-ESS2-2)","The wide sandy beaches, 160 to 210 metres, are all in the bays where the rock is soft, and the headlands have no beach or only boulders. Soft rock wears back faster, which makes the bay and supplies the sand that collects in it. (4-ESS2-2)","It will stick out into the sea with little or no beach, because every granite point in the chart does. The reasoning from the pattern is what is being checked. (4-ESS2-2)"]},{std:["4-ESS3-2"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the two plans. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"generating and comparing solutions that reduce the impact of a natural Earth process",html:()=>`<p class="big">The cliff is wearing back about 70 centimetres a year and the beach path is now close to the edge. The town can fund one plan.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Plan</th><th>What it does</th><th>Cost</th><th>What else it changes</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: pile large rock along the cliff base</td><td>takes the force of the waves so the base is not undercut</td><td>high</td><td>the beach in front narrows over time, and the next stretch of cliff erodes faster</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: move the path 20 metres inland and plant the cliff face</td><td>accepts the erosion and keeps the path out of its way; roots slow the face</td><td>medium</td><td>a strip of park land is given up to the cliff over time</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name a third plan of your own for the cliff path.","Compare plan A and plan B. Which would you fund, and what in the chart supports your choice?","The cliff wears back about 70 centimetres a year. Compare the two plans for how long each one keeps the path clear of the edge. Then say which one you would still be glad of in twenty years."],key:["Any workable idea: build a boardwalk that can be taken up and moved, add sand to widen the beach so waves do not reach the base, drain water out of the cliff top so the face is less likely to slump, fence off the edge and reroute the path in stages. (4-ESS3-2)","Either plan, judged on the chart. Plan A protects that one stretch but costs the most, narrows the beach and speeds the erosion next door. Plan B costs less and works with the erosion rather than against it, but gives up land over time. Noticing that plan A moves the problem along the coast is the strongest reasoning. (4-ESS3-2)","Twenty years at 70 centimetres a year is about 14 metres of cliff, so a path moved 20 metres inland is still clear of the edge at the end of it, while the rock at the base has to hold for the whole twenty years and the chart says the beach in front of it narrows as it does. Either plan may be named as the one to be glad of, and what is rated is that the rate is used against the time rather than the chart being read back. Or: a student who works it as 7 metres in ten years, or who says the path would last about 28 years at that rate, is reasoning the same way. Partial: a plan named with no use of the 70 centimetres a year is approaching. (4-ESS3-2)"]}]},{id:20,title:"Summer Power at the Beach Town",short:"Energy Sources",parts:[{std:["4-ESS3-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"describing where energy and fuels come from and how their use affects the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">Demand for electricity is highest on hot summer afternoons. The town's four sources are in the chart.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Source</th><th>Natural resource behind it</th><th>Runs out?</th><th>When it gives the most</th><th>One effect on the environment</th></tr>
     <tr><td>gas power station</td><td>natural gas from underground</td><td>yes</td><td>any time it is running</td><td>gases into the air</td></tr>
     <tr><td>rooftop solar</td><td>sunlight</td><td>no</td><td>midday and early afternoon</td><td>panels made and later recycled</td></tr>
     <tr><td>coastal wind turbines</td><td>moving air</td><td>no</td><td>late afternoon and evening, when the sea breeze picks up</td><td>noise, and a hazard for birds</td></tr>
     <tr><td>diesel backup generator</td><td>oil from underground</td><td>yes</td><td>only when it is switched on</td><td>exhaust and noise</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Trace two of these sources back to the natural resource they start from.","Solar gives the most at midday and wind in the late afternoon. Explain why using both together suits a hot summer day.","The town adds wind turbines and runs the gas station less. Name one effect that gets smaller and one new effect that appears."],key:["Any two read correctly: the gas station from natural gas underground, rooftop solar from sunlight, the turbines from moving air, the generator from oil underground. (4-ESS3-1)","Demand stays high from midday into the evening. Solar covers the midday peak and fades as the sun drops, and the sea breeze picks up in the late afternoon just as the solar falls away, so between them they cover more of the day than either could alone. (4-ESS3-1)","Fewer gases from the gas station. New effects: turbine noise along the coast and a hazard for birds, and on a still day the turbines give nothing so the gas station runs again. (4-ESS3-1)"]}]},{id:21,title:"The Boardwalk Pucks",short:"Speed",parts:[{std:["4-PS3-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the results. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"using evidence to explain how the speed of an object relates to its energy",html:()=>`<p class="big">The same puck was pushed along the boardwalk three times into a block of foam, and the block was pushed along in front of it.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Push</th><th>Speed of the puck</th><th>Foam block moved</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>1 metre each second</td><td>3 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>3 metres each second</td><td>24 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>5 metres each second</td><td>66 cm</td></tr></table>`,qs:["What happens to the distance the foam block moves as the puck's speed goes up?","Which push had the most energy in the moving puck? Name the evidence.","From push 1 to push 3 the speed went up five times. Did the block move five times as far? Say what that shows."],key:["It grows, and by larger steps each time: 3 cm, 24 cm, 66 cm. (4-PS3-1)","Push 3, at 5 metres each second, because it moved the block 66 cm, farther than either other push. (4-PS3-1)","No, it moved about twenty two times as far, from 3 cm to 66 cm, while the speed went up only five times. Energy grows much faster than speed. Noticing the mismatch is the point of this item. (4-PS3-1)"]},{std:["4-PS3-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud or in writing. A prediction needs a reason, not the right answer.",desc:"asking a question and predicting the energy change in a collision",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two pucks of the same size sit on the smooth boardwalk. A heavy metal puck is sliding toward a light plastic puck that is standing still.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("coin",96)}${F.icon("beachBall",96)}</div>`,qs:["Ask one question about this collision that a test could answer.","Predict what happens to each puck after they meet.","The same collision is tried on dry sand instead of the boardwalk. Predict what is different and say why."],key:["Any testable question: how far does the plastic puck slide, does the click get louder with a faster slide, what happens if the plastic puck is sliding too, does a felt pad on the front change the result. (4-PS3-3)","The plastic puck is pushed forward and slides away quickly. The metal puck slows down and may stop. Both halves make the full prediction. (4-PS3-3)","Everything travels a much shorter distance on sand, because the sand rubs and shifts and takes energy out of the pucks as heat and as moving grains, so less is left for motion. (4-PS3-3)"]}]},{id:22,title:"Energy at the Kiosk",short:"Transfer",parts:[{std:["4-PS3-2"],n:4,dur:75,dir:"For each observation, say how the energy travelled. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"matching observations to energy transferred by sound, light, heat or electric current",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four things noticed at the beach kiosk on a hot afternoon.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>What was noticed</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>A metal spoon left in the pot of hot water becomes too hot to hold at the handle.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>A lead runs from the socket to the ice cream freezer, and the motor starts up.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>The kiosk bell rings and a family at the water's edge turns round.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>The low sun shines through the kiosk window and a bright stripe lands on the back shelf.</td></tr></table>`,qs:["How did the energy travel in observation 1?","How did it travel in observation 2?","How did it travel in observation 3?","How did it travel in observation 4?"],key:["By heat, from the hot water into the metal and along the spoon to the handle. (4-PS3-2)","By an electric current, through the lead from the socket to the freezer motor, where it becomes motion and sound. (4-PS3-2)","By sound, through the air from the bell across the sand to their ears. (4-PS3-2)","By light, from the sun through the window to the shelf. (4-PS3-2)"]},{std:["4-PS3-4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Draw your device and label it. Then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"designing, testing and improving a device that converts energy from one form to another",html:()=>`<p class="big">The kiosk wants a light on its sign at dusk. No cable may be run along the sand. Design a device that turns one kind of energy into electrical energy to run the light. You may use a float on a lever moved by the waves. You may use a propeller in the sea breeze. You may also use a small generator, wire, a hand crank, or a sunlight panel.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("wave",96)}${F.icon("lamp",96)}</div>`,qs:["Draw and label your device. Show the energy going in and the electrical energy coming out.","Name the kind of energy going in and the kind coming out.","You test it and the light comes on in bursts instead of staying on. Name one change and say why it should help."],key:["Any labelled drawing with a path: a float rising and falling on the waves, working a lever that turns a small generator wired to the light; a propeller in the breeze turning a generator; a sunlight panel wired in. Labels and a path make it a design. (4-PS3-4)","Going in: motion of the waves or the breeze, or light from the sun. Coming out: electrical energy, plus some heat and sound. Both forms named. (4-PS3-4)","Any change with a reason: add a battery or capacitor that stores energy between waves and feeds the light steadily, add a flywheel so the generator keeps spinning between pushes, gear it so a slow wave still spins the generator fast. (4-PS3-4)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Ocean Waves, Water and Flags",short:"Waves",parts:[{std:["4-PS4-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the two drawings. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"using a wave model to describe amplitude and wavelength and the effect waves have on objects",html:()=>`<p class="big">A float is anchored just off the beach. The lifeguard drew the sea on a calm morning and again on a breezy afternoon.</p>
     <div class="figure"><svg viewBox="0 0 520 300" width="520" role="img" aria-label="Two sea surface waves drawn above one another with letters A and B marking parts of the first">
       <text x="10" y="18" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif">calm morning</text>
       <line x1="20" y1="70" x2="500" y2="70" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 6"/>
       <path d="M20 70 q30 -24 60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0" fill="none" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="5"/>
       <line x1="50" y1="70" x2="50" y2="58" stroke="var(--coral)" stroke-width="4"/>
       <text x="62" y="64" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif">B</text>
       <line x1="20" y1="112" x2="140" y2="112" stroke="var(--coral)" stroke-width="4"/>
       <text x="80" y="134" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif" text-anchor="middle">A</text>
       <text x="10" y="186" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif">breezy afternoon</text>
       <line x1="20" y1="240" x2="500" y2="240" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 6"/>
       <path d="M20 240 q15 -62 30 0 t30 0 t30 0 t30 0 t30 0 t30 0 t30 0 t30 0 t30 0 t30 0 t30 0 t30 0 t30 0 t30 0 t30 0 t30 0" fill="none" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="5"/>
     </svg></div>`,qs:["Which letter marks the amplitude, and which marks the wavelength?","Compare the two drawings. Say what happened to the amplitude and what happened to the wavelength.","What does the breezy afternoon do to the anchored float?"],key:["B marks the amplitude, from the flat line up to the crest. A marks the wavelength, from one crest to the next. (4-PS4-1)","The amplitude got bigger and the wavelength got smaller. The crests are taller and much closer together on the breezy afternoon. (4-PS4-1)","The float rises and falls farther and more often, because the taller waves carry more energy and the shorter wavelength means they arrive more frequently. It still bobs on its anchor rather than travelling along with the waves. (4-PS4-1)"]},{std:["4-PS4-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud or in writing. You may draw arrows.",desc:"modelling how light reflects from objects into the eye so the objects can be seen",html:()=>`<p class="big">A shell lies on the sand under shallow clear water and can be seen easily. The same shell is put in a bucket with a lid on, and now it cannot be seen at all.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("shell",96)}${F.icon("sun",96)}</div>`,qs:["Draw or tell the path of the light when someone sees the shell under the shallow water. Name each place it goes.","Inside the closed bucket the shell has not changed. Why can nobody see it?","Dark sunglasses make the beach look dimmer. Explain how, using the path of the light."],key:["Light leaves the sun, travels down through the water to the shell, reflects off the shell, travels back up through the water, and travels into the eye. All the legs are needed, and noticing the light passes through the water both ways is a strong answer. (4-PS4-2)","No light gets inside the closed bucket, so there is none to reflect off the shell and reach an eye. An object must send light to the eye to be seen. (4-PS4-2)","The lenses sit in the path between the beach and the eye and let only some of the light through, so less light reaches the eye and everything looks dimmer. (4-PS4-2)"]},{std:["4-PS4-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the two systems. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"comparing two solutions that use patterns to send information",html:()=>`<p class="big">The two ends of the beach need three messages: all clear, come to the tower, and the water is closed for the day.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>System</th><th>How a message is sent</th><th>Needs</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Coloured flag on the tower</td><td>a green flag, a yellow flag, or two flags together</td><td>daylight and a clear view along the sand; nobody needs to be listening</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Air horn</td><td>one blast, two blasts, or three blasts</td><td>heard over the surf for about 500 metres; reaches people facing the water</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Say what the pattern is in each system.","Name one job each system does better than the other.","Design a third system of your own for the same three messages."],key:["In the flag system the pattern is the colour shown and how many flags are flying. In the horn system it is the number of blasts. An agreed pattern standing for a message is what carries the information. (4-PS4-3)","The flag can be checked at any moment by anybody who looks, and it stays up, so a late arrival sees it too. The horn reaches people who are facing the water or not looking, and works when the view along the sand is blocked. (4-PS4-3)","Any pattern with three agreed signals: one, two or three lamp flashes at dusk, a board with one, two or three shapes on it, three positions of an arm or a paddle, three agreed words on a radio. (4-PS4-3)"]}]},{id:24,title:"A Cooler for the Kiosk",short:"Design",parts:[{std:["3-5-ETS1-1"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Read the situation, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria for success and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">The kiosk has no electricity for a fridge. The drinks in the crate are warm by midday. There is one morning to build something, a stack of cardboard boxes, old towels, foil, a tub, and a bag of ice each day.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("box",88)}${F.icon("drop",88)}</div>`,qs:["State the problem in one sentence, in your own words.","Write two criteria that would tell the kiosk the design works.","Write two constraints from the situation."],key:["Something like: the drinks need to stay cold until the end of the afternoon without electricity. A problem statement, not a solution. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any two testable criteria: drinks below 10 degrees at four in the afternoon, one bag of ice lasts the whole day, a customer can reach a drink in a few seconds, it fits behind the counter. (3-5-ETS1-1)","One morning, only boxes, towels, foil, a tub and one bag of ice a day, and no electricity. Any two. A criterion written here instead is approaching. (3-5-ETS1-1)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Study the three designs. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"generating and comparing solutions against the criteria and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three designs for the cooler.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>What it is</th><th>Materials</th><th>Build time</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>the tub of ice inside a box, with the gap packed with towels and the lid foil covered</td><td>box, towels, foil, tub</td><td>short</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>a box inside a box with towels between the walls, foil on the outside, and the tub of ice inside</td><td>two boxes, towels, foil, tub</td><td>most of the morning</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>a small electric cooler plugged into a socket</td><td>a cooler to buy, and power</td><td>order and wait</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which design breaks a constraint? Name the constraints it breaks.","Compare designs 1 and 2 against the criteria. Which do you expect to keep the ice longer, and why?","Add a fourth idea of your own using only what is on hand."],key:["Design 3. It has to be bought, the kiosk has no electricity, and it would not arrive within the one morning. Any of those constraints named. (3-5-ETS1-2)","Design 2, because two walls with towels between them, and foil outside to send the sunlight back, slow the heat getting in from every side. Design 1 has only one wall and a single packed gap, so heat reaches the ice faster. Look for the idea of slowing heat from outside. (3-5-ETS1-2)","Any idea in the materials given: wrap the whole tub in towels and then in foil, keep the cooler in the shade behind the counter, add a second lid so less warm air gets in when it is opened, raise the box off the hot ground on a crate, use a small opening instead of a whole lid. (3-5-ETS1-2)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"planning a fair test with controlled variables and naming a failure point",html:()=>`<p class="big">The kiosk wants to find out which cooler keeps drinks colder through the day. Both are built and there is a thermometer.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("thermometer",96)}${F.icon("box",96)}</div>`,qs:["Plan the test. What will you measure, and when?","Name two things you must keep the same for both coolers so the test is fair.","Name one failure point: where your cooler is most likely to fail first."],key:["Measure the drink temperature inside each cooler at set times, for example every hour from nine until four, and weigh or measure how much ice is left at the end. Numbers at matching times, not impressions. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Any two: the same amount of ice at the same starting temperature, the same number and kind of drinks, the same spot and the same shade, the same number of times each lid is opened, the same day and weather. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Any sensible weak spot: the cardboard goes soggy from the melt water and collapses, the towels get wet and stop slowing the heat, the lid does not seal, the foil tears, meltwater has nowhere to drain and sits around the drinks. (3-5-ETS1-3)"]}]},{id:25,title:"California Ports",short:"Regions",parts:[{std:["HSS-4.1"],n:4,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart of four California ports. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"describing the physical and human geographic features that define places and regions in California",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four ports along the California coast, and the land behind each of them.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("anchor",80)}${F.icon("sailboat",80)}${F.icon("wave",80)}</div>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Port</th><th>Natural shelter</th><th>Region behind it</th><th>What moves through it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>San Francisco Bay</td><td>a large bay behind a narrow strait</td><td>the northern Central Valley by river and rail</td><td>containers, grain, machinery</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Los Angeles and Long Beach</td><td>an open bay with long breakwaters built by people</td><td>the southern cities and the desert by road and rail</td><td>the largest container traffic in the country</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Monterey Bay</td><td>a wide curved bay with a deep canyon offshore</td><td>coastal farm valleys</td><td>fishing boats, research vessels, vegetables by road</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Humboldt Bay</td><td>a long shallow bay behind sand spits</td><td>the northern forests</td><td>timber, fishing boats</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which port's shelter is mostly built by people rather than given by nature? Explain the difference.","Why would a deep natural bay matter to a region that grows crops inland?","Humboldt Bay is behind the northern forests and moves timber. Name the physical feature and the human activity in that sentence.","Pick any port and explain how the land behind it shapes what moves through it."],key:["Los Angeles and Long Beach. The bay there is open to the ocean, so long breakwaters were built to make calm water for ships. The others sit behind a strait, a curve of coast or sand spits that nature provided. Partial: the right port named with no account of what the others have instead is approaching. (HSS-4.1)","Crops have to reach buyers far away, and ships carry the most for the least cost. A deep sheltered bay lets big ships load safely, so the inland region can sell beyond California. Partial: saying ships are cheaper with nothing said about the depth or shelter of the bay is approaching. (HSS-4.1)","Physical feature: the northern forests, and the bay behind the sand spits. Human activity: cutting and shipping timber, and running fishing boats. Partial: one of the two named is approaching. (HSS-4.1)","Any port with a sensible link: San Francisco Bay ships valley grain brought down by river and rail; Los Angeles and Long Beach serve huge cities and inland routes so containers dominate; Monterey moves vegetables from the nearby farm valleys; Humboldt moves timber from the forests behind it. Partial: a port named with no link to the land behind it is approaching. (HSS-4.1)"]}]},{id:26,title:"By Sea to California",short:"California Past",parts:[{std:["HSS-4.2"],n:3,dur:180,dir:"Read the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"describing the life and interactions among the peoples of early California, on the coast and inland",html:()=>`<p class="big">The ocean in the life of early California.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>People and period</th><th>How they used the sea</th><th>Who they dealt with</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Chumash, for thousands of years</td><td>plank canoes sewn and sealed with tar, able to cross to the Channel Islands; fishing and shellfish gathering</td><td>island and mainland villages traded with each other, using shell beads as money</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Spanish missions, 1769 onward</td><td>supply ships from the south brought tools, cloth and seed to the coastal missions</td><td>Franciscan missionaries and Spanish officials, and Native Californians who did the work at the missions</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Mexican ranchos, 1820s onward</td><td>hides and tallow were carried out to ships anchored offshore in small boats</td><td>traders from the eastern United States and Europe</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The sea was part of Chumash religious belief as well as Chumash work. Chumash accounts of how their world began place the first people on one of the Channel Islands, so the crossing led to a place that mattered for more than fishing. Their ceremonies were led by trained specialists, and the shell beads that served as money were used in those ceremonies too. Other California nations had their own separate beliefs and ceremonies rather than sharing the Chumash ones.</p>
     <p class="big">Spain sent the Franciscan missionaries by sea to found the missions. Their purpose was to convert Native Californians to Christianity and to teach them Spanish crops and trades, and the missions they built stood on land Native Californian nations had held.</p>`,qs:["The Chumash crossed open water to the Channel Islands. What does that tell you about their boats and their knowledge? According to the note, the islands meant something besides a place to fish. What else did they mean?","According to the chart and the note, answer these. What were the Franciscan missionaries sent to California to do? Why did the missions depend on supply ships rather than on roads?","Compare who the Chumash traded with and who the ranchos traded with."],key:["Their plank canoes were seaworthy enough for open ocean and they knew the winds, currents and weather well enough to cross and return. That takes both skilled building and deep knowledge of the sea. On the second half: Chumash accounts of how their world began place the first people on one of the Channel Islands, so the islands held religious meaning and were not only fishing grounds. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so the boats and knowledge with nothing on the second half sits there. (HSS-4.2)","They were sent to convert Native Californians to Christianity and to teach them Spanish crops and trades. On the ships: there was one road, El Camino Real, and it was slow, long and had no carts for heavy loads over much of it. Ships could carry far more weight at once and reached the coastal missions directly, which is where the missions were built. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so naming the purpose with nothing on the ships sits there. (HSS-4.2)","The Chumash traded between their own island and mainland villages and with neighbouring nations, inside California. The ranchos traded across the ocean with traders from the eastern United States and Europe. One network was local and long established, the other reached across the world. Partial: one of the two networks described is approaching. (HSS-4.2)"]},{std:["HSS-4.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"explaining the Gold Rush and the road to statehood",html:()=>`<p class="big">San Francisco in the Gold Rush years.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1846</td><td>Settlers at Sonoma declared the Bear Flag Republic, which ended within weeks when the war with Mexico reached California.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1848</td><td>The war ended and California passed to the United States. Gold was found at Coloma.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1849 to 1850</td><td>Hundreds of ships arrived and were left in the harbour, because their crews walked off to the gold fields.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>What happened to the ships</td><td>Some were used as stores, hotels and a jail. Others were sunk on purpose and built over as the city filled in the shoreline.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1850</td><td>California became the 31st state. San Francisco had grown from a village of a few hundred into a city of about 25,000.</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Why were so many ships abandoned in the harbour?","The city used old ships as buildings and then built over them. What does that tell you about San Francisco in 1850?","Name two changes the Gold Rush brought to California. Then say which one you think mattered longest."],key:["The crews left to look for gold, so there was nobody to sail the ships back. A sailor could earn far more in the gold fields than at sea, and captains could not hire replacements. Partial: saying the crews went for gold with nothing said about why nobody replaced them is approaching. (HSS-4.3)","It was growing far faster than it could build. Housing, stores and even a jail were needed immediately, so anything already floating in the harbour was put to use, and the shoreline itself was filled in to make more land. Partial: describing the ships as buildings with nothing said about the speed of growth is approaching. (HSS-4.3)","Any two with a reason: the population multiplied, statehood came in 1850, cities and ports grew, mining changed the rivers and harmed farming downstream, Native Californians lost land and food sources, people arrived from all over the world and shaped the state's make up. The judgement about which lasted longest is the student's, as long as it is reasoned. Partial: two changes named with no judgement about which lasted longest is approaching. (HSS-4.3)"]}]},{id:27,title:"Trade Today, and Who Governs the Coast",short:"Government",parts:[{std:["HSS-4.4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"tracing California's development into an agricultural and industrial power since the 1850s",html:()=>`<p class="big">How California's goods reached buyers, over time.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Period</th><th>How goods moved</th><th>What that allowed</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1850s</td><td>sailing ships around South America, and river boats inland</td><td>wheat and hides could be sold far away, but the trip took months</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1869 onward</td><td>the transcontinental railroad</td><td>goods reached eastern cities in about a week</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1880s onward</td><td>refrigerated railcars</td><td>fresh fruit and vegetables could be sold across the country</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1950s onward</td><td>trucks on new highways, and container ships</td><td>goods moved door to door, and trade across the Pacific grew quickly</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which change in the chart mattered most to a fruit grower, and why?","Containers came in from the 1950s. Why would a box that fits a ship, a train and a truck change trade so much?","Name one effect these changes had on where people in California worked."],key:["Refrigerated railcars, because fruit spoils in days. Without cooling a grower could only sell nearby, and with it the whole country became a market. Partial: a change named with no reason it mattered most is approaching. (HSS-4.4)","The same box can be lifted straight from ship to train to truck without unpacking, so loading is far faster and cheaper and less is damaged or lost. That made long distance trade worth doing for many more goods. Partial: saying it is faster with nothing said about moving between ship, train and truck is approaching. (HSS-4.4)","Any supported effect: work grew at the ports and in warehousing and trucking, packing houses grew near the fields and the railroads, farm towns grew along the shipping routes, and port cities grew fastest of all. Partial: an effect asserted with no change from the chart behind it is approaching. (HSS-4.4)"]},{std:["HSS-4.5"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"explaining the structures, functions and powers of local, state and federal government",html:()=>`<p class="big">Who governs the coast.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Place or job</th><th>Level in charge</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the city beach and its lifeguards</td><td>local, the city</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a state beach and state park</td><td>state</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a national park or national seashore</td><td>federal</td></tr>
     <tr><td>rules about fishing in ocean waters far from shore</td><td>federal</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the city rule about dogs on the sand</td><td>local, the city council</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a court case about whether a coast rule is allowed</td><td>state or federal courts</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Three different levels look after beaches in this chart. Name a beach for each level.","Why would rules about fishing far from shore be federal rather than local?","Name the branch of government that would handle the last row. Then say what that branch does."],key:["Local: the city beach with city lifeguards. State: a state beach and state park. Federal: a national park or national seashore. Partial: two of the three levels matched to a beach is approaching. (HSS-4.5)","Waters far from shore are not inside any one city or state, and fish move across all of those lines. One set of rules for the whole country is the only way to manage them, so it is a federal job. Partial: saying it is a federal job with no reason is approaching. (HSS-4.5)","The judicial branch, the courts. Courts decide what a law means and whether a rule is allowed under the constitution, in a case brought before them. Partial: the branch named with no account of what it does is approaching. (HSS-4.5)"]}]}];const WC=FLU.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;const readPart=STOPS[1].parts[0];readPart.n=WC;readPart.key[0]=readPart.key[0].replace("__WC__",WC);readPart.desc=`a ${WC}-word grade-level story read aloud`;const LEVELUPS={3:{id:"L3",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Exact Words",std:["RL.5.1"],needs:["RL.4.1"],n:2,dir:"Find the words in the story and read them out loud exactly as they are written.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Read me the exact words from the story that show Kwame could not read the canoe from the bow seat.</li><li>The story never says that Kwame was glad to be told he was a good bow. Read me the exact words that show it anyway.</li></ol>${F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true)}`,key:["Quotes accurately, such as: “In the bow you see nothing but lake,” or “Kwame could not tell when the canoe was about to turn.” The student should read the words as written rather than retell them. (RL.5.1, quoting accurately)","Quotes: “Kwame turned around to coil the rope so nobody would see him grinning, which did not work at all.” Grinning and hiding it are the evidence. (RL.5.1, quoting to back an inference)"]},4:{id:"L4",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Three Texts",std:["RI.5.9"],needs:["RI.4.9"],n:2,dir:"Read the third text. Then answer out loud using all three texts.",html:()=>`${threeTexts()}<ol class="qlist"><li>Use all three texts. Explain the whole chain: what the readings at Pine Lake have done over ten summers, what that probably means about the water, and why the readings cannot name a cause by themselves.</li><li>Which text would you use to find out how the number is measured, which to find out what Pine Lake's numbers have done, and which to explain what clouds a lake? Tell why.</li></ol>`,key:["Text 3: the summer averages at Pine Lake fell from 4.6 meters in the first summer to 3.1 meters in the tenth. Text 2: a smaller clarity reading means the disk disappears nearer the surface, and a row of summers is what shows a real change rather than month-to-month wandering. Text 1: cloudier water usually means more algae, which grows faster with more phosphorus, and the phosphorus arrives from the land. Text 3 also says why the readings cannot name a cause: two gravel roads were paved and a farm stopped leaving a planted strip, and the numbers cannot say which change mattered. (RI.5.9)","Text 2 for how it is measured, because it is the only one that describes the disk and the marked rope. Text 3 for Pine Lake's own numbers and the ten year trend. Text 1 for what clouds a lake, because it explains phosphorus and algae. (RI.5.9)"]},8:{id:"L8",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Paired Words",std:["L.5.1"],needs:["L.4.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each sentence out loud the correct way.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Finish it with either and or, or with neither and nor: ____ the spare paddle ____ the bailer was in the canoe. The canoe was empty.</li><li>By the time the bell rang, the crew ____ for two hours. (paddle) Start with had been.</li><li>Fix the shift in tense: Last night the crew carried the canoes up, stacks them on the rack, and ties the tarp down.</li></ol>`,key:["Neither the spare paddle nor the bailer was in the canoe. (L.5.1, correlative conjunctions)","had been paddling. (L.5.1, past perfect progressive tense)","Last night the crew carried the canoes up, stacked them on the rack, and tied the tarp down. (L.5.1, correcting a shift in verb tense)"]},12:{id:"L12",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Mixed Units",std:["5.MD.A.1"],needs:["4.MD.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>A rope is 250 centimeters long. How many meters is that?</li><li>The crew has three ropes: one 4 meters, one 250 centimeters, and one 1.2 meters. What is the total length in meters?</li><li>A water jug holds 2,500 milliliters. How many liters is that?</li></ol>`,key:["2.5 meters. 250 ÷ 100 = 2.5. (5.MD.A.1, converting a smaller unit to a larger one)","7.7 meters. 250 centimeters is 2.5 meters, and 4 + 2.5 + 1.2 = 7.7. (5.MD.A.1, a multi-step problem that needs a conversion first)","2.5 liters. 2,500 ÷ 1,000 = 2.5. (5.MD.A.1)"]},14:{id:"L14",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Unlike Bottoms",std:["5.NF.A.1"],needs:["4.NF.A.2"],n:3,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>2/5 + 1/3 = ?</li><li>7/8 − 1/6 = ?</li><li>The cooks used 5/6 of a jug in the morning and 3/4 of a jug at lunch. How much did they use in all?</li></ol>`,key:["11/15. In fifteenths that is 6/15 + 5/15. (5.NF.A.1, adding fractions with unlike denominators)","17/24. In twenty-fourths that is 21/24 − 4/24. (5.NF.A.1)","19/12 of a jug, which is 1 7/12 jugs. In twelfths that is 10/12 + 9/12. (5.NF.A.1)"]},15:{id:"L15",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Thousandths",std:["5.NBT.A.3"],needs:["4.NF.C.7"],n:3,dir:"Say number 1 out loud. Write the other two on your paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Read this gauge reading out loud: 2.405</li><li>Write three and nineteen thousandths as a decimal.</li><li>Put these in order from least to greatest: 0.7, 0.07, 0.77, 0.707</li></ol>`,key:["Two and four hundred five thousandths. “Two point four zero five” on its own does not meet the standard, which asks for the number name. (5.NBT.A.3)","3.019. Nineteen thousandths fills the hundredths and thousandths places, so a zero holds the tenths place. (5.NBT.A.3)","0.07, 0.7, 0.707, 0.77. Written to three places they are 0.070, 0.700, 0.707, and 0.770. (5.NBT.A.3)"]}};const PASSAGE_STD=["RL.4.1","RL.4.4","RI.4.1","RI.4.9","W.4.8","W.4.2","RL.5.1","RI.5.9"];const MULT_N=12;const FRAC_DENS=[1,2,3,4,5,6,8,12];let multSel=[0,0],fracOn={},pvVal="",gridOn=[0,0];function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}const PV_COLS=["Ten thousands","Thousands","Hundreds","Tens","Ones","Tenths","Hundredths","Thousandths"];function pvTable(){const raw=pvVal.replace(/[^0-9.]/g,""),dot=raw.indexOf(".");const whole=(dot<0?raw:raw.slice(0,dot)).slice(-5),frac=(dot<0?"":raw.slice(dot+1)).slice(0,3);let h="<tr>";PV_COLS.forEach(c=>h+=`<th>${c}</th>`);h+="</tr><tr>";for(let i=0;i<5;i++){const k=whole.length-(5-i);h+=`<td>${k>=0?whole[k]:""}</td>`}for(let i=0;i<3;i++)h+=`<td>${i<frac.length?frac[i]:""}</td>`;return h+"</tr>"}function pvHtml(){return`<label class="pv-in">Type a number<input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="pvInput" value="${pvVal}" autocomplete="off"></label><div class="tool-scroll"><table class="pv" id="pvTable">${pvTable()}</table></div><p class="small">Each place to the left is worth 10 times as much. Each place to the right is worth one tenth as much.</p>`}function fracHtml(){return FRAC_DENS.map(d=>{const on=fracOn[d]||0;let r=`<div class="fs-row">`;for(let k=0;k<d;k++)r+=`<button class="fs-part${d>=10?" sm":""}${k<on?" on":""}" data-d="${d}" data-k="${k}">${d===1?"1 whole":"1/"+d}</button>`;return r+"</div>"}).join("")+`<p class="small">Click a piece to shade up to it. Click the last shaded piece to clear the row.</p>`}function gridHtml(){return`<div class="hg-wrap">`+[0,1].map(g=>{let c=`<div><div class="hg">`;for(let i=0;i<100;i++)c+=`<button class="hg-c${i<gridOn[g]?" on":""}" tabindex="-1" data-g="${g}" data-i="${i}" aria-label="Grid ${"AB"[g]} square ${i+1}"></button>`;return c+`</div><p class="fig-label" style="margin:4px 0 0">Grid ${"AB"[g]}</p></div>`}).join("")+`</div><p class="small">Each grid is 1 whole. Click a square to shade up to it. One full row of ten is one tenth. Click the last shaded square to clear.</p>`}function unitHtml(){return F.table(["1 of the bigger unit","equals","of the smaller unit"],[["1 kilometer","=","1,000 meters"],["1 meter","=","100 centimeters"],["1 kilogram","=","1,000 grams"],["1 pound","=","16 ounces"],["1 liter","=","1,000 milliliters"],["1 hour","=","60 minutes"],["1 minute","=","60 seconds"],["1 foot","=","12 inches"],["1 yard","=","3 feet"]])+`<p class="small">Going from a bigger unit to a smaller one, multiply. The count gets bigger because each unit is smaller.</p>`}function areaHtml(){return F.table(["What to find","How"],[["Area of a rectangle","length × width, answer in square units"],["Perimeter of a rectangle","2 × (length + width), answer in plain units"],["A missing side length","area ÷ the side you already know"],["How many fence pieces","perimeter ÷ the length of one piece"]])+`<p class="small">Area counts the squares inside the shape. Perimeter measures the trip around the outside.</p>`}function shapeHtml(){return F.table(["Word","What it means"],[["Parallel sides","run the same direction and never meet, however far they go"],["Perpendicular sides","meet at a square corner"],["Right angle","a square corner, like the corner of this page"],["Right triangle","a triangle with one right angle"],["Parallelogram","four sides, two pairs of parallel sides"],["Trapezoid","four sides, exactly one pair of parallel sides"]])+`<p class="small">Use the corner of a card or a folded paper to test whether an angle is a right angle, narrower, or wider.</p>`}const STARTERS={opinion:["Camp should pick ____ because ____.","One reason is ____.","For example, ____.","Also, ____.","That is why ____."],facts:["Clarity is ____.","To measure it, ____.","For example, ____.","One reading is not enough because ____.","So ____."],story:["On the last morning, ____.","First, ____.","After that, ____.","“____,” said ____.","By the end, ____."],"W.4.8":["Text 1 says ____.","Text 2 says ____.","In my own words, ____.","My sources: ____ and ____."],"RI.4.9":["Text 1 says ____.","Text 2 says ____.","Put together, that means ____."],"RL.4.4":["The word ____ means ____.","I know because the story says ____."],"SL.4.2":["In my own words, the report said ____.","The problem was ____.","What camp did was ____.","The graph shows ____."],"SL.4.4":["I am going to tell you about ____.","One thing to know is ____.","For example, ____.","The last thing is ____."]};const SENT_ALIAS={opinion:"W.4.1",facts:"W.4.2",story:"W.4.3","W.4.1":"opinion","W.4.2":"facts","W.4.3":"story"};function sentKey(st){const ps=st&&st.parts&&st.parts.length?st.parts:st?[st]:[];const pick=c=>!c?null:STARTERS[c]?c:SENT_ALIAS[c]&&STARTERS[SENT_ALIAS[c]]?SENT_ALIAS[c]:null;for(const p of ps){const a=pick(p.alt&&p.alt.key||p.wk||p.wt);if(a)return a;for(const c of p.std||[]){const k=pick(c);if(k)return k}}return null}function sentHtml(st){const k=sentKey(st);return`<ul class="starters">${(STARTERS[k]||STARTERS["W.4.8"]).map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}const TOOLS={mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:["4.OA.A.1","4.NBT.A.1","4.MD.A.1","4.MD.A.3","4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","5.MD.A.1","5.NF.A.1"],html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const rr=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===rr&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[rr,k];r()})},pv:{name:"Place value chart",std:["4.NBT.A.1","4.NBT.A.3","4.NF.C.6","4.NF.C.7","5.NBT.A.3"],html:pvHtml,bind:b=>{const inp=b.querySelector("#pvInput"),tb=b.querySelector("#pvTable");if(inp&&tb)inp.oninput=()=>{pvVal=inp.value.replace(/[^0-9.]/g,"");tb.innerHTML=pvTable()}}},frac:{name:"Fraction strips",std:["4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","5.NF.A.1"],html:fracHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll(".fs-part").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const d=+x.dataset.d,k=+x.dataset.k;fracOn[d]=fracOn[d]===k+1?0:k+1;r()})},grid:{name:"Hundredths grids",std:["4.NF.C.6","4.NF.C.7"],html:gridHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll(".hg-c").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const g=+x.dataset.g,i=+x.dataset.i;gridOn[g]=gridOn[g]===i+1?0:i+1;r()})},units:{name:"Measurement unit card",std:["4.MD.A.1","5.MD.A.1"],html:unitHtml},area:{name:"Area and perimeter card",std:["4.MD.A.3"],html:areaHtml},shapes:{name:"Shape word card",std:["4.G.A.2"],html:shapeHtml},sent:{name:"Sentence frames",std:["W.4.1","W.4.2","W.4.3","W.4.8","RI.4.9","RL.4.4","SL.4.2","SL.4.4"],html:sentHtml}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"mult",std:["4.OA.A.1"],msg:"The multiplication chart supplies the facts, so rate on whether the student can say the comparison in words and write it as an equation, not on the fact itself."},{t:"mult",std:["4.NBT.A.1","4.MD.A.1","4.MD.A.3","4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","5.MD.A.1","5.NF.A.1"],msg:"The multiplication chart gives the products. Rate on the setup, the unit, and the reasoning."},{t:"pv",std:["4.NBT.A.1"],msg:"The place value chart names the places and lines the digits up, so the student does not have to recall place names. The ten times reasoning still has to come from the student."},{t:"pv",std:["4.NBT.A.3"],msg:"The chart shows which digit sits in the place being rounded to, so count the rounding as supported, not independent."},{t:"pv",std:["4.NF.C.6","4.NF.C.7","5.NBT.A.3"],msg:"The chart lines the decimal places up for the student, which is most of the work in comparing decimals. Count these answers as supported."},{t:"frac",std:["4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","5.NF.A.1"],msg:"Fraction strips show the piece sizes and let the student line two fractions up, so rate on whether the student can explain what the strips show rather than on the comparison itself."},{t:"grid",std:["4.NF.C.6","4.NF.C.7"],msg:"The hundredths grids turn a decimal into a picture the student can count, so count the writing and comparing as supported, not independent."},{t:"units",std:["4.MD.A.1","5.MD.A.1"],msg:"4.MD.A.1 expects the student to know the relative sizes of the units. With the unit card on the screen, this stop shows only whether the student can pick the right operation. Note it on the record sheet."},{t:"area",std:["4.MD.A.3"],msg:"The card gives both formulas, the missing-side method, and the fence method, so count these answers as supported, not independent."},{t:"shapes",std:["4.G.A.2"],msg:"The shape word card defines parallel, perpendicular, right angle, parallelogram, and trapezoid, so the naming questions become supported. Rate on whether the student can find those features on the signs."},{t:"sent",std:["W.4.1","W.4.2","W.4.3"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["W.4.8"],msg:"The frames show how to label a note and name a source, so count the list of sources as supported."},{t:"sent",std:["RI.4.9","RL.4.4","SL.4.2","SL.4.4"],msg:"The frames tell the student where to pull from and how to open. Rate on the content of the answer rather than on its shape."},{t:"passage",std:["RL.4.1","RL.4.4","RI.4.1","RI.4.9","W.4.8","W.4.2","RL.5.1","RI.5.9"],msg:"If the passage was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:PASSAGE_STD}}});
